  Kept verbatim for reference only.  Four separate findings in the
  Entity &amp; Spawn System notebook say this approach cannot be made
  safe from here:

  1. "Live enemy type-swapping crashes.  Editing the table BEFORE
     spawn is safe (S4)."  These loops ran every 100 ms during
     gameplay, which is live by definition.

  2. "Set-piece records must not be retyped.  008E6298 (Town East,
     record 0, the scripted first monster) crashes the encounter."
     lvl2TownEast[1] below is exactly 008E6298.

  3. "The spawn table is the WRONG safety source."  A level's table
     stages the day and night rosters together, so a table entry is
     no proof the assets are resident.  The correct source is the
     per-area character registry at 01F7D820, which Mayhem already
     reads and this script never did.

  4. "The registry is per-AREA, and type IDs shift per map."  These
     loops wrote raw IDs into thirteen tables at once regardless of
     which level was loaded, so a level never visited this session
     was already dirty before it loaded.  The writes are into static
     .data in a no-ASLR image and nothing here ever restored them.

